---
import PrimerChat from "../PrimerChat.astro";
import { aiCardId, archiveAi } from "../../../lib/archive-ai";
import { run } from "../../../lib/first-run";
import { spell } from "../../../lib/cases";

const card = archiveAi["prove-it"];
const boxes = spell(run.boxes);
const others = spell(run.boxes - 1);

type Artifact = { id: string; name: string; rec: string; used: boolean | null; changed?: boolean };
const artifacts: Artifact[] = [
  { id: "report", name: "Citywide report", rec: "Meter readings snapshot → unit conversion → monthly totals", used: true, changed: true },
  { id: "dash", name: "Dashboard download", rec: "Citywide report's monthly totals → export", used: true, changed: true },
  { id: "count", name: "Meters reporting, by month", rec: "Converted readings → count of meters that reported", used: true, changed: false },
  { id: "inventory", name: "Building inventory", rec: "Facilities register → inventory build, no unit conversion", used: false },
  { id: "unknown", name: "A download with no manifest", rec: "No record", used: null },
];
const yesNo = (b: boolean | undefined) => (b === undefined ? "Not replayed" : b ? "Yes" : "No");
const action = (a: Artifact): [string, string, string] =>
  a.used === null ? ["Unknown", "is-warn", "Hold for investigation."]
  : !a.used ? ["Unaffected", "is-ok", "Leave alone: a separate pipeline."]
  : a.changed ? ["Republish", "is-bad", "Replayed, and its published figures changed."]
  : ["Keep", "is-ok", "Replayed with identical published content: record the verification."];

const agentPrompt = `Fix the watt-hour to kilowatt-hour conversion bug in the city electricity
reporting pipeline and stage a correction for the January–March releases.

1. Inventory the published artifacts. For each artifact, read the build manifest
   or verified provenance record. Identify its source snapshot, relevant step
   versions and settings, and upstream artifact fingerprints. Trace downstream
   dependencies from the faulty conversion. Classify each artifact as affected,
   unaffected, or unknown. Explain each classification using the recorded
   provenance. Hold unknown artifacts for investigation.

2. Replay affected builds in staging using their original input snapshots and
   pinned dependencies, with the conversion fix applied. Validate the conversion
   and reconcile the rebuilt totals against the source readings. Compare the
   rebuilt artifacts with the published versions, ignoring generation timestamps
   and other incidental build metadata. Record which user-facing values changed.

3. Propose republication for affected artifacts whose published content changed,
   including downstream reports or downloads with changed values. Leave
   unaffected artifacts and artifacts with identical published content in place.
   Preserve the original releases in the archive and propose versioned
   replacements for the corrected artifacts.

4. Draft a correction note that names the affected releases, explains the unit
   error, gives verified before-and-after figures, and identifies where readers
   can get the corrected files. Include what readers should replace if they used
   the original figures.

5. Before publishing, report the root cause and fix, each artifact's classification
   and supporting fingerprint evidence, the replay inputs, validation results,
   before-and-after figures, unresolved provenance gaps, and the exact artifacts
   proposed for replacement. Include the staged files and the draft correction
   note. Stop and wait for my approval before replacing any published artifact or
   sending the notice.`;

const fresh = spell(run.schedules - 1);

const [briefIntro, ...briefSteps] = agentPrompt.split(/\n\n/).map((p) => p.replace(/\s*\n\s*/g, " ").trim());

const checks = [
  ["No manifest", "A February download has no build manifest, but its totals look plausible. How should the agent classify it, and why?"],
  ["Same figures", "A report’s fingerprint shows it used the faulty conversion, but the replay produces identical published figures and content. What should the agent do with the report?"],
  ["Before approving", "What evidence should you ask the agent to show before approving republication, and which parts of that evidence belong in the public correction note?"],
];
---

<PrimerChat
  prompt={card.prompt}
  module="Module 2"
  back={`../02-archive/#${aiCardId("prove-it")}`}
  about="This is a reply an AI assistant gave to Module 2's recovery-plan study prompt, lightly edited. Send the same prompt yourself and you will get a different reply."
  turn={`In a live chat, you would answer those three questions and the assistant would review your reasoning. To try that, copy the prompt into your own AI assistant. Then go back to Part 8 and ${card.returnTask.replace("Then come back and ", "")}`}
>
  <h2 class="pr-title">From recovery plan to agent prompt</h2>
  <p>Use fingerprints to decide <strong>what needs replay</strong>, then compare the replayed output with the published version to decide <strong>what needs a correction</strong>.</p>
  <p>Suppose a city discovers in July that its January–March electricity reports treated some meter readings in watt-hours as though they were already in kilowatt-hours. The published package includes a citywide report, a dashboard download, and a separate inventory of city buildings.</p>

  <h2>1. What to replay and republish</h2>
  <p>Start with the archived inputs and a manifest of what actually produced each published artifact. A useful fingerprint identifies the source snapshot, the versions of the processing steps that ran, their relevant configuration, and the upstream artifacts they consumed. Use those recorded dependencies to trace the faulty conversion through the published outputs. Replay every affected build from its original input snapshot, with the conversion fixed and the other dependencies pinned. Hold any artifact whose provenance is missing or inconclusive for investigation.</p>
  <p>Then compare each rebuilt artifact against the published version. Compare the data people use, such as CSV values or report totals, independently of incidental differences such as a fresh generation timestamp. Republish the report and dashboard download if their figures changed. Leave the building inventory alone if its fingerprint shows that it came from a separate pipeline.</p>
  <p class="pr-pull">If a replayed artifact has identical published content, record the verification and keep the existing publication.</p>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-cls" aria-live="polite">
    <fieldset class="pr-seg">
      <legend>Classify one artifact</legend>
      {artifacts.map((a, i) => <label><input type="radio" name="pa-rec-art" value={a.id} checked={i === 0} /><span>{a.name}</span></label>)}
    </fieldset>
    {artifacts.map((a) => {
      const [verdict, tone, why] = action(a);
      return (
        <div class="pa-cls-view" data-art={a.id}>
          <ul class="pa-cls-steps">
            <li><span>Provenance record</span><b>{a.rec}</b></li>
            <li><span>Used the faulty conversion?</span><b>{a.used === null ? "Can't tell" : a.used ? "Yes" : "No"}</b></li>
            <li><span>Replay changed published values?</span><b>{a.used ? yesNo(a.changed) : "Not replayed"}</b></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="pa-cls-verdict"><span class:list={["pr-chip", tone]}>{verdict}</span> {why}</p>
        </div>
      );
    })}
    <figcaption>Provenance decides what to replay. The replay's comparison decides what to republish. The meter count shows the difference: stale, but unchanged.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>2. What to tell people who used it</h2>
  <p>A correction note should identify the affected releases, name the figures that changed, explain the cause, and point readers to the corrected versions. For example, if the replay confirmed these results:</p>
  <blockquote class="pa-note">
    <p><strong>Correction to the January–March city electricity reports</strong></p>
    <p>We corrected the monthly electricity totals in the citywide report and dashboard download. The original releases overstated consumption because some meter readings in watt-hours were counted as kilowatt-hours. The March total changed from 2.8 million kWh to 1.9 million kWh. The corrected files include revised figures for all three months. If you used the original figures, replace them with the corrected release.</p>
  </blockquote>

  <h2>3. Finished prompt for the coding agent</h2>
  <figure class="pr-card pa-brief">
    <p class="pr-label">Prompt</p>
    <p>{briefIntro}</p>
    <ol>{briefSteps.map((st) => <li>{st.replace(/^\d+\.\s*/, "")}</li>)}</ol>
  </figure>
  <aside class="pr-course" aria-label="Course note">
    <p class="pr-label">Course note</p>
    <p>The reply's “fingerprint” covers an artifact's whole provenance. In this course it is a hash of one parser's code and config. A parsed file is stale when that fingerprint or the content hash of its page has changed.</p>
    <p>In Part 8 the box-score parser's fingerprint changed, so all {boxes} parsed Alder Ridge box scores are stale, not only the one with overtime, while the {fresh} schedules stay fresh, like the city's separate building inventory. The other {others} box scores are the reply's meter count: stale, replayed, unchanged, so nothing to republish.</p>
    <p>One gap in the reply's sample note: it never says where the corrected release is, though its own rule asks for that. A real note links it.</p>
  </aside>

  <h2>4. Check your understanding</h2>
  <ol class="pr-checks">{checks.map(([name, q]) => <li><b>{name}</b><span>{q}</span></li>)}</ol>
</PrimerChat>

<style>
  .pa-cls .pr-seg { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }
  .pa-cls-view { display: none; }
  .pa-cls:has([value="report"]:checked) [data-art="report"],
  .pa-cls:has([value="dash"]:checked) [data-art="dash"],
  .pa-cls:has([value="count"]:checked) [data-art="count"],
  .pa-cls:has([value="inventory"]:checked) [data-art="inventory"],
  .pa-cls:has([value="unknown"]:checked) [data-art="unknown"] { display: block; }
  .pa-cls-steps { display: grid; gap: 0.35rem; margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-cls-steps li { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; justify-content: space-between; gap: 0.2rem 0.8rem; padding: 0.4rem 0.65rem; border-radius: 6px; background: var(--tl-bg); }
  .pa-cls-steps span { color: var(--tl-muted); }
  .pa-cls-steps b { color: var(--sl-color-white); font-weight: 600; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-cls-verdict { margin: 0.6rem 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  .pa-note { margin: 0 0 1rem; padding: 0.7rem 0.95rem; border: 1px solid var(--tl-line); border-left: 4px solid var(--pr-yellow); border-radius: 8px; background: var(--tl-card); font-style: normal; }
  :global(.pr) .pa-note p:last-child { margin: 0; }
  .pa-note strong { color: var(--sl-color-white); }
  .pa-brief { border-left: 3px solid var(--pr-ai); }
  :global(.pr) .pa-brief p { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--sl-color-white); font-weight: 600; }
  .pa-brief ol { display: grid; gap: 0.45rem; margin: 0; padding-left: 1.3rem; font-size: 0.92rem; }
</style>
